ent :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00534dc3-4006-5bef-9575-96f837e9a134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453840} :answer-field/id #uuid "44a3b668-b559-550b-9651-be66b31864e6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7df593d-9767-5ee4-9c1c-cf41d12d4aeb" :question/math-academy-id "q-221932" :question/problem "Which of the following statements must be true for any integers $x$ and $y?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $6$ divides $(24x+30y)$\n- **II.** $3$ divides $(24x+30y)$\n- **III.** $5$ divides $(24x+30y)$" :question/worked-solution "The notation $a∣b$ means $a$ divides $b.$\n\nAccording to the definition of integer divisibility, the fact that $a$ divides $b$ means that there exists an integer $k$ such that \n\n$$\nb=ak.\n$$\n\n \nWith that in mind, let's examine our statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n24x+30y=6(4x+5y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $4x+5y$ must be an integer, too. So, $6$ divides $(24x+30y).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that \n\n$$\n24x+30y=3(8x+10y).\n$$\n\nSince $x$ and $y$ are integers, $8x+10y$ must be an integer, too. So, $3$ divides $(24x+30y).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, if $x=-1$ and $y=1,$ we have $24(-1)+30(1)=6,$ which is not divisible by $5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186407606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198261 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d6db336-845a-506a-b020-cced1b38e23a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Laws Involving Implications and Equivalences" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048574 :topi