6473564} {:answer/id #uuid "515754aa-883f-54ef-9b07-757603046125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "515754aa-883f-54ef-9b07-757603046125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473565} :answer-field/id #uuid "2dfe423e-2133-5abd-80c5-484043bc3e63" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474177}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3fb6cc00-0fe3-5564-afaa-6bddf30c9f68" :question/math-academy-id "q-90955" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\nI. $f:[0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$\n\nII. $g:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$\n\nIII. $h:R\\to [0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={e}^{x}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is an into function. Let's take $-1\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ such that $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}=-1.$ In fact, $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- II is a *not* an into function. Given any  $y\\in [0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x=\\sqrt{y}\\in R.$ Then $g(x)=g(\\sqrt{y})=(\\sqrt{y})^{2}=y.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $0\\in [0,\\infty ).$ There is no number $x\\in R$ such that $h(x)={e}^{x}=0.$ In fact, ${e}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01ad9700-5aaa-5b00-8d2f-acce6ed59c9a" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is the Set of Real Numbers" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051069 :topic/id #uuid "7e5753ab-fcea-5d2f-ac49-4b475153e05c" :topic/math-academy-id 3027 :topic/title "Into Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f4b9fce-88a2-5c65-8752-31bb8c08b10d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454090} {:answer/id #uuid "1c77a378-a0f8-5768-8e57-d661a10c42dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f4b9fce-88a2-5c65-8752-31bb8c08b10d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454090} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae3c14ef-feb4-50b1-b173-09d5116caffe" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454795} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fd58017-e4ba-5b47-983d-1e652dcc9e5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454092} {:answer/id #uuid "d49b29d6-d5d4-51be-84b7-33db7b3b848e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d49b29d6-d5d4-51be-84b7-33db7b3b848e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454093} :answer-field/id #uuid "35de4aba-fc6c-5fa8-a181-9eb17458aad4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454796} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00096cbf-a739-5274-b425-3e305c30dad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454094} {:answer/id #uuid "80dd2edb-196d-5524-841f-8e00d6c94a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00096cbf-a739-5274-b425-3e305c30dad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454094} :answer-field/id #uuid "df7bb5e2-b8b1-56ec-86e5-324f98f8a216" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4110bb56-279f-50bf-96a1-301ebf1eb3ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-90859" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ which is given by $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The range of $f$ is $(0,\\infty )$\n\n- **II.** $∀\\,y\\in R$ $∃\\,x\\in (0,\\infty )$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\n- **III.** $f$ maps $(0,\\infty )$ **into** $R$" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/5e/5e3e074a2d029aeb3f4016332b9fb762fff6ec60ed7dce0290f97a95d6cb57c1.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. From the graph, there is no highest or lowest $y$ value. The graph will cover any real value $y.$ So the range of the function is all $R..$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$, then for all $y$ contained within $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ there exists some $x$ contained within $R$ such that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,\\infty )$ the function is a surjection. Therefore, $f$ is *not* an into function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410012 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051069 :topic/id #uuid "7e5753ab-fcea-5d2f-ac49-4b475153e05c" :topic/math-academy-id 3027 :topic/title "Into Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c93ca70a-47da-5baf-851c-e1bf791c7b9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453174} {:answer/id #uuid "64807ced-aa57-58a5-ae0d-ac029c59ac34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64807ced-aa57-58a5-ae0d-ac029c59ac34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453175} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b4fceb5-932c-5132-bb87-d2badbb9ddb9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454337} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6c61cf70-41be-5318-bf2e-74949ba6f18f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453176} {:answer/id #uuid "236cd33b-d523-510a-84cc-440d53a1b398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6c61cf70-41be-5318-bf2e-74949ba6f18f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453176} :answer-field/id #uuid "39af1c63-d29d-5ade-b582-b9d3d244610e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454338} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ef1019c-6076-59b2-b8b1-dfd04b1ded3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453178} {:answer/id #uuid "3d6c31df-bd28-538d-b442-6e9f77cc33df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ef1019c-6076-59b2-b8b1-dfd04b1ded3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453178} :answer-field/id #uuid "5543a8bb-3812-58e8-8791-6e292fe8e1f3" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454339}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b62af08-9109-5599-b006-f2ceb899c9fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-105263" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:R\\to (-\\infty ,1)$ which is given by $f(x)=1-{2}^{x}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The range of $f$ is $(-\\infty ,1)$\n\n- **II.** $∀\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,1],$ $∃\\,x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y$\n\n- **III.** $f$ is a function of $R$ **into** $(-\\infty ,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we graph the function.\n\n![](images/35/35bd21eef06d1ea63aba6e420d9842de61f9248c4fa26c2895f6825f4e7a3848.png)\n\nRecall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. From the graph, the function takes all $y$-values lower than $y=1$, excluding $y=1$ itself. So the range of the function is indeed $(-\\infty ,1).$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since the range of the function is $(-\\infty ,1)$,  there can't be an $x\\in R$ whose image is $1\\notin (-\\infty ,1).$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement I is true, the function is a surjection.  So, $f$ is *not* a function of $R$ *into* $(-\\infty ,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051069 :topic/id #uuid "7e5753ab-fcea-5d2f-ac49-4b475153e05c" :topic/math-academy-id 3027 :topic/title "Into Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe1a6204-e5ec-5f44-b26e-7ccc55915e0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473542} {:answer/id #uuid "ae086f9f-b92c-5aab-8c76-5c009e5e2e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae086f9f-b92c-5aab-8c76-5c009e5e2e82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473543} :answer-field/id #uuid "9131fc67-75e4-563c-b8d0-94a23a385951" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474166} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "791d06be-d390-539a-ac0c-06e35b4d0cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473544} {:answer/id #uuid "fad30099-8449-5c4a-8a50-0b3446240d14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "791d06be-d390-539a-ac0c-06e35b4d0cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473544} :answer-field/id #uuid "d37b982e-6668-5b7d-b9a0-36b334e068a8" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474167} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa15fe7e-8ae5-55d8-95d5-857c052ca9bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473546} {:answer/id #uuid "0098fe9d-5f1e-546e-a0ec-56802952db84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa15fe7e-8ae5-55d8-95d5-857c052ca9bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473546} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d089faf-62fc-5a4e-ad21-0bf3a5219729" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae359058-a9c8-58ca-9940-1c14f3b8e228" :question/math-academy-id "q-90789" :question/problem "![](images/7f/7f68e70397b982a70b372b2bd9e4f73da3374d3abfb2ef4bbd2ec1fffe6dbb31.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\nI. $f(2)=4$\n\nII. $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n\nIII. The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,   there is an arrow from $2\\in X$ to $4\\in Y.$ So,  $f(2)=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, $f$ is *not* an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd378899-03ff-57a0-848c-11b93c8e5055" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Whose Codomain Is a Real Interval" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051069 :topic/id #uuid "7e5753ab-fcea-5d2f-ac49-4b475153e05c" :topic/math-academy-id 3027 :topic/title "Into Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc41b65-ba1b-56a8-b545-ecd45b401396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473554} {:answer/id #uuid "da2eb5f7-1baa-53f4-ad79-0d1d55247851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bc41b65-ba1b-56a8-b545-ecd45b401396" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473554} :answer-field/id #uuid "74710deb-3884-595d-a92d-aa098b3250f9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474172} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3b0aebe-c007-541d-b409-83571ec2c6e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473556} {:answer/id #uuid "07e54daa-2cf8-5d76-b9fc-ac27c1ee1152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3b0aebe-c007-541d-b409-83571ec2c6e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473556} :answer-field/id #uuid "d55e82c7-9120-59da-8986-81de94c17491" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474173} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d8197f8-ea17-52e0-ada2-12a7ef638766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473558} {:answer/id #uuid "579573c7-4c0d-580e-a0b2-b9f7c31c308f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "579573c7-4c0d-580e-a0b2-b9f7c31c308f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473559} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f071064-8b41-5c17-83e0-821190cb7676" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d76a2452-4488-5b99-ace5-042b8edc218d" :question/math-academy-id "q-90902" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are **into** functions?\n\nI. $f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=-{log}_{2}⁡(x)$\n\nII. $h:R\\to (0,\\infty )$ defined by $h(x)={3}^{x}$\n\nIII. $g:Q\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- I is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in R,$ we can choose $x={2}^{-y}\\in (0,\\infty ).$ Then $f(x)=f({2}^{-y})=-{log}_{2}⁡({2}^{-y})=y.$\n\n- II is a *not* an into function. Given $y\\in (0,\\infty ),$ we can choose $x={log}_{3}⁡y\\in R.$ Then $h(x)=h({log}_{3}⁡y)={3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(y)}=y.$\n\n- III is an into function. Let's take $y=\\sqrt[3]{(\\sqrt{2})}\\in R.$ There is no number $x\\in Q$ such that $g(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}=y.$ Indeed, in this case we would have \n\n$$\n\\sqrt[3]{x}=y\\,⇔\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}=\\sqrt[3]{(\\sqrt{2})}\\,⇔\\,x=\\sqrt{2}\\in Q,\n$$\n\n which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6aa86a7e-d5b2-518a-8c43-8e6136bcf900" :knowledge-point/title "Functions Defined as Mapping Diagrams" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051069 :topic/id #uuid "7e5753ab-fcea-5d2f-ac49-4b475153e05c" :topic/math-academy-id 3027 :topic/title "Into Functions"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "532ae303-67bd-57f2-938f-bbed36329b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472478} {:answer/id #uuid "fb500e02-911c-5c8b-9f78-e88f6e8c72fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472479}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "532ae303-67bd-57f2-938f-bbed36329b1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472478} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cec2b1e-72b6-50a1-855d-2902cfa078a9" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473634} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7073623d-5ce7-509c-a188-db7013873ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472480} {:answer/id #uuid "09b1b2c0-e961-5267-b086-8cb73af100c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09b1b2c0-e961-5267-b086-8cb73af100c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472481} :answer-field/id #uuid "d770e4eb-8dda-5703-95a9-2c2d291bcb35" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473635} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11bb4560-454b-5e25-81d8-5d5901fcb0f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472482} {:answer/id #uuid "42ef5f9d-dc43-5572-9241-468f3a9c6c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472483}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42ef5f9d-dc43-5572-9241-468f3a9c6c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472483} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b741a97-1773-51a6-a938-029c480ebbf0" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e45af0c1-a25f-5254-88c0-adcdabddd288" :question/math-academy-id "q-105246" :question/problem "![](images/ae/ae980a05fc0d25d2696185c88fcf90ff156af9c3f42af7cece80f8c33692a3d7.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $f:X\\to Y$ represented by the mapping diagram above?\n\nI. $f(1)=7$\n\nII. $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y$\n\nIII. The function $f:X\\to Y$ is **into**\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. There is no arrow from $1\\in X$ to $7\\in Y.$ So, $f(1)\\ne 7.$\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the diagram, $5\\in Y$ has no preimage in $X$ because we have no arrows from $X$ to $5\\in Y.$ As a result, there exists some $y$ (i.e., $y=5$) contained within $Y$ such that $f(x)\\ne y$ for any $x$ in $X.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, $f$ is an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a5d11c2-4a7c-5fdc-a363-8a87b90db19e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Dimension of a Span by Inspection" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048218 :topic/id #uuid "893a2f73-ff77-57dc-a5aa-12aa27975a06" :topic/math-academy-id 1866 :topic/title "The Dimension of a Span"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ae48163-0998-5b3d-b29e-8d844bd75e73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453454} {:answer/id #uuid "fac7d0b5-081a-56e0-901b-a2ae495d04aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ae48163-0998-5b3d-b29e-8d844bd75e73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453454} :answer-field/id #uuid "d31b9f46-6bca-5c00-88d4-25fd24f38387" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454477} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8a92446-e8c8-5739-82ef-d0ba00e62ae7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453456} {:answer/id #uuid "00c70672-536d-5c0e-84ab-327beb9e056e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00c70672-536d-5c0e-84ab-327beb9e056e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453457} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d6e49be-3808-5326-8880-c9624d3a62b4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454478} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d46f9ac4-f8c7-5cc1-9675-07387c704c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453458} {:answer/id #uuid "325a6d83-0f48-5758-9374-3733e1e6f1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d46f9ac4-f8c7-5cc1-9675-07387c704c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453458} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ea1fc63-07a4-50e6-a7e4-57b3bf645806" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454479}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a087057-8a12-50fd-9bce-b13921276b0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-159782" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ be linearly independent vectors, and suppose that the vector $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ form a basis of $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$\n\n- **II.** $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\})=2$\n\n- **III.** $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=2$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that a basis of a vector space $V$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that span $V.$ The dimension of $V$ is the number of vectors in any basis of $V$ (if such a basis exists). \n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span.\n\n- Statement II is true. We are told that $w$ can be written as a linear combination of ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}.$ This means that $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\}$ is completely spanned by the vectors ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}$ only. So, $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},w\\})=2.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, $\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$, and we are told that ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ are linearly independent vectors. So, ${v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}$ is a basis of the span. Therefore, we have $\\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}\\})=3$ (not $2$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152263 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfbebc20-1b48-5718-a057-b743c8af8de1" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Defined on the Set of Real Numbers" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048139 :topic/id #uuid "82869b1d-f321-5919-b531-5dbc3fc95c4d" :topic/math-academy-id 2679 :topic/title "Bijections"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2fffb5d-3a48-5bb6-ac88-ce930684ca97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473434} {:answer/id #uuid "294eeec8-975d-5c82-ae0f-2771583b8dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "294eeec8-975d-5c82-ae0f-2771583b8dca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473435} :answer-field/id #uuid "dd849a69-1d9a-56c7-b5dd-51c95b3a7fb1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474112} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "840247a1-44e4-5764-9cf1-c049a173b2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473436} {:answer/id #uuid "946cdfda-8e89-55b0-9fa0-8e85f564674b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473437}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "946cdfda-8e89-55b0-9fa0-8e85f564674b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473437} :answer-field/id #uuid "73a56616-2123-5fa5-b737-ce96e6534c3c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474113} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba3e6678-4e3d-5c51-a908-8193a93dce9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473438} {:answer/id #uuid "2fed4611-09f6-5910-ade6-a7e1facbbba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473439}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fed4611-09f6-5910-ade6-a7e1facbbba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473439} :answer-field/id #uuid "2124b191-2881-520e-896a-bfc3832d55f9" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474114}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "00868f73-2a29-5bc5-b5fa-287f2fcfd175" :question/math-academy-id "q-82304" :question/problem "![](images/ef/ef3933fc56e981bd0ef6e88754c46f5ab00b15230cce5fc2ea224ecf7c1f8f0e.png)\n\nFor the function $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=2\\sqrt[3]{x}$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](images/f0/f020379f306770bd87fc499ac1fef238dc6aa639fb36d5229502fcd7dd455610.png)\n\n        \n\n- Statement II is true. According to the graph, the range of $f(x)$ will cover the entire interval $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true because the function is both surjective and injective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048139 :topic/id #uuid "82869b1d-f321-5919-b531-5dbc3fc95c4d" :topic/math-academy-id 2679 :topic/title "Bijections"} :activity/title "Bijections" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410903} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410940}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152265 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f237343d-2f8a-5a9f-9e5f-c9f22784547a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Functions Defined Algebraically" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048139 :topic/id #uuid "82869b1d-f321-5919-b531-5dbc3fc95c4d" :topic/math-academy-id 2679 :topic/title "Bijections"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91e829b3-51c9-553b-9a85-45a365a040d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472772} {:answer/id #uuid "25d4d0b8-e87c-5870-9268-7bd36f45c2be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91e829b3-51c9-553b-9a85-45a365a040d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472772} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab2f2b46-2c6e-5eb1-85c3-ad99eab4b5bb" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473781} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b76c9ff3-fe58-5372-b648-22e1105f9edc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472774} {:answer/id #uuid "dc0aa8d4-59ff-53f5-b71d-5399b2d3aa82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc0aa8d4-59ff-53f5-b71d-5399b2d3aa82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472775} :answer-field/id #uuid "de3d2d3d-6e53-548d-8c99-3c996253ee94" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473782} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c7d6de4-ec23-5a7c-93c0-50dd18473fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472776} {:answer/id #uuid "2f38aaba-83e0-5b28-9931-c911d3ca9a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c7d6de4-ec23-5a7c-93c0-50dd18473fef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472776} :answer-field/id #uuid "577e1483-33f9-5dc2-81fd-fee7d86b3dcf" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6f4dc780-0892-5640-8a66-1c76dbdf69ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-170667" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n- **I.** $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$\n- **II.** $g:R\\to R$ defined by $g(x)=2x$\n- **III.** $h:Z\\to Z$ defined by $h(x)=2x$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Remember that the range of $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ This means that \n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, for example, there is no value $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=4.$\n\n- II is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in R,$ we can choose an $x\\in R$ such that $g(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(x) & =y \\\\ 2x & =y \\\\ x & =\\frac{y}{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that\n\n$$\ng(x)=g(\\frac{y}{2})=2(\\frac{y}{2})=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g({x}_{1}) & =g({x}_{2}) \\\\ 2{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $g$ is both surjective and injective, $g$ is bijective.\n\n- III is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. Notice that if $x$ is an integer number, then $h(x)=2x$ is an even number. This means that the range of $h$ is the set of even numbers.\nSo, for example, there is no value $x\\in Z$ such that $h(x)=3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048139 :topic/id #uuid "82869b1d-f321-5919-b531-5dbc3fc95c4d" :topic/math-academy-id 2679 :topic/title "Bijections"} :activity/title "Bijections" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410903} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410941}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152264 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12e21e5-0a96-5f87-9642-d63d23e0038a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Whose Domains or Codomains Are Real Intervals" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048139 :topic/id #uuid "82869b1d-f321-5919-b531-5dbc3fc95c4d" :topic/math-academy-id 2679 :topic/title "Bijections"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46ba97c8-22ce-5f2e-a361-5e8cdc04dd4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472484} {:answer/id #uuid "b27c6df3-9636-5cec-a7c6-7e29c522a025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472485}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b27c6df3-9636-5cec-a7c6-7e29c522a025" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472485} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb62bcbf-8eae-5cf3-b5ae-4a506ba9980e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473637} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa5400a8-4d69-5498-bb26-cf35113f0a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472486} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a7df63-ed8d-5f4d-8c4c-c2ea8c9ec5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3a7df63-ed8d-5f4d-8c4c-c2ea8c9ec5ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472487} :answer-field/id #uuid "08bfc292-3bc3-54f3-9448-43fbcbae4c11" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473638} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc48bae5-5a50-5365-8135-13ed7b0935d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472488} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d6054e-cf6e-54d1-a1b7-c5a2fedc0e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8d6054e-cf6e-54d1-a1b7-c5a2fedc0e20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472489} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b1b7116-3a54-5f89-a498-e625b68c4215" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "80ecff06-aa66-5d3f-b38e-164bc959ec2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-105321" :question/problem "![](images/63/63032bf65ec10d55f9a0b968a7179db934ad6f17b869d50999e4a0f710be9977.png)\n\nFor the function $f:(-1,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](images/71/71bd13b2dd618fb60cd2e7f6c2e3807f3449e233ccb9b60e1cbd14cc928345b3.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. According to the graph, the range of $f(x)$ is $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is true because the function is both surjective and injective.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048139 :topic/id #uuid "82869b1d-f321-5919-b531-5dbc3fc95c4d" :topic/math-academy-id 2679 :topic/title "Bijections"} :activity/title "Bijections" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410903} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410943}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152265 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f237343d-2f8a-5a9f-9e5f-c9f22784547a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Functions Defined Algebraically" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048139 :topic/id #uuid "82869b1d-f321-5919-b531-5dbc3fc95c4d" :topic/math-academy-id 2679 :topic/title "Bijections"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e823525c-d068-59c5-b79c-15a3ccf71c59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473362} {:answer/id #uuid "891e6926-8a5d-5e45-a792-62f1489010a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "891e6926-8a5d-5e45-a792-62f1489010a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473363} :answer-field/id #uuid "19b384f0-5c30-5d34-9679-cd7e07ed517c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474076} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0389deac-290e-5e2b-a79a-47bb5723e7bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473364} {:answer/id #uuid "2a3a48bf-eee6-5f4b-abad-5b2daa66506b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0389deac-290e-5e2b-a79a-47bb5723e7bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473364} :answer-field/id #uuid "410ca3ae-a20d-51b7-85fe-4743d9e9e5fd" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474077} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92d7f5c0-1fd8-56a4-9076-f3007ffc482a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473366} {:answer/id #uuid "25dd17ba-ad09-5a8f-ab00-e55bf3b14ffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473367}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92d7f5c0-1fd8-56a4-9076-f3007ffc482a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473366} :answer-field/id #uuid "c95d975f-eefe-5a67-a299-22eea80c5b71" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f181e810-d779-5077-8c9b-e3a1b446edcd" :question/math-academy-id "q-77480" :question/problem "Which of the following functions are bijections?\n- **I.** $f:R\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=x+1$\n- **II.** $g:Q\\to R$ defined by $g(x)={x}^{2}$\n- **III.** $h:Z\\to Q$ defined by $h(x)={2}^{x}$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\n- I is a bijection. First of all, it is a surjection because $∀\\,y\\in R,$ we can choose an $x\\in R$ such that $f(x)=y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =y \\\\ x+1 & =y \\\\ x & =y-1\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe verify that, \n\n$$\nf(x)=f(y-1)=(y-1)+1=y.\n$$\n\nSecond, it is an injection: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f({x}_{1}) & =f({x}_{2}) \\\\ {x}_{1}+1 & ={x}_{2}+1 \\\\ {x}_{1} & ={x}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is both surjective and injective, $f$ is bijective.    \n\n- II is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. As a counterexample, choose $y=2.$ Then there is no rational number $x$  such that $g(x)=2.$ The only numbers $x$ that satisfies ${x}^{2}=2$ are $x=\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\notin Q.$\n\n- III is *not* a bijection because it is not surjective. As a counterexample, choose $y=0.$ Then there is no integer $x$  such that $g(x)=0,$  because ${2}^{x}>0$ for all $x\\in Z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048139 :topic/id #uuid "82869b1d-f321-5919-b531-5dbc3fc95c4d" :topic/math-academy-id 2679 :topic/title "Bijections"} :activity/title "Bijections" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410903} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410939}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410938 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152264 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12e21e5-0a96-5f87-9642-d63d23e0038a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Bijections For Functions Whose Domains or Codomains Are Real Intervals" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048139 :topic/id #uuid "82869b1d-f321-5919-b531-5dbc3fc95c4d" :topic/math-academy-id 2679 :topic/title "Bijections"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac4098b-5192-54c8-8f6d-e195dab81971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473440} {:answer/id #uuid "41e127f3-fa89-57cb-ad00-c33a0ad41569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41e127f3-fa89-57cb-ad00-c33a0ad41569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473441} :answer-field/id #uuid "2de55556-81c5-5e7d-8e52-b17b07e64f9e" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474115} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d987b394-ce7e-50ee-bd9e-15b2865507e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473442} {:answer/id #uuid "b94f447a-92fa-59ce-915e-6bc43de8b1cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d987b394-ce7e-50ee-bd9e-15b2865507e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473442} :answer-field/id #uuid "18762654-245a-5a4b-9f7e-5b1cdc56bc3f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474116} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "594452de-a12a-52ba-853d-a92da8d4b8eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473444} {:answer/id #uuid "af604334-e529-5439-bf59-b34bae36264b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "594452de-a12a-52ba-853d-a92da8d4b8eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473444} :answer-field/id #uuid "68b84854-48a5-5f9e-bc3e-762fc29560f5" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f636c031-3e1e-5fb8-bd29-acadcf4da72e" :question/math-academy-id "q-82572" :question/problem "![](images/ce/ce68b2f27b88bd43d6eebfa5380a22c00e15e4194e0ad45e6778f761667a3ceb.png)\n\nFor the function $f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=x-2$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](images/2f/2ff4c0183b91aa23d780f665aa0f038b374583e9f24e245951c4c69ea5f2e5b8.png)\n\n           \n\n- Statement II is false. There is no value $x$ for which $f(x)=-2\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\n Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048139 :topic/id #uuid "82869b1d-f321-5919-b531-5dbc3fc95c4d" :topic/math-academy-id 2679 :topic/title "Bijections"} :activity/title "Bijections" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410903} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410942}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411065 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d56b033-a2c8-57c7-adde-e574d726ec78" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Symbolic Conditional Statements" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049805 :topic/id #uuid "155403ed-fbfa-59a9-b928-e762a73145ef" :topic/math-academy-id 247 :topic/title "Logical Equivalence with Conditional Statements"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e80d5d8d-2b17-5886-9b55-a7b81e348575" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453622} {:answer/id #uuid "eb698ee6-4b77-5b14-90cb-e74df6b1abac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453623}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb698ee6-4b77-5b14-90cb-e74df6b1abac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453623} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ab0edef-ad32-52ce-a777-7ef518d62f83" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454561} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8de87b1-d8ef-50e0-94f7-a2832adb4959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453624} {:answer/id #uuid "f7312739-063d-5aef-8534-6f2ac7974c84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d8de87b1-d8ef-50e0-94f7-a2832adb4959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453624} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9edf5f2-ac8c-5b69-99b2-3ae4c084b126" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454562} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54641cb5-3584-597c-bcb0-67da0251b5b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453626} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7d916c-501c-57d8-ad96-61e5a1dc8fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453627}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a7d916c-501c-57d8-ad96-61e5a1dc8fde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453627} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2b4fa69-fdf7-5386-8328-31ecb2099e55" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454563}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c7ec246a-1c3b-5830-ae08-ed99cbcc20da" :question/math-academy-id "q-182503" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $A⇒B≡¬A∨B$\n- **II.** $¬(A⇒B)≡A∨¬B$\n- **III.** $¬(A⇒B)≡A∧¬B$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A⇒B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬A∨B.$\n\n- Statement III is true, while statement II is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nA⇒B≡¬A∨B\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(A⇒B)≡¬(¬A∨B).\n$$\n\nDistributing the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law, we have\n\n$$\n¬(¬A∨B)≡¬(¬A)∧¬B.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nA∧¬B.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(A⇒B)$ is equivalent to $A∧¬B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411117 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157231 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc5b8688-3838-5e9e-9304-9a4c787034ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Logically Equivalent Statements" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050777 :topic/id #uuid "65e4f73e-43a8-5089-914d-135e7396b995" :topic/math-academy-id 2821 :topic/title "Logical Equivalence"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ff3ba28-38e8-51bc-a126-2eff2903bff1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472574} {:answer/id #uuid "2d8239a8-397c-5bf4-8f09-ac6412a0e0bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472575}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d8239a8-397c-5bf4-8f09-ac6412a0e0bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472575} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d4328ce-d94c-50ad-9bc6-66e180d0c379" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473682} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de4aeae3-2eea-55f8-9d3b-f6f28669d75a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472576} {:answer/id #uuid "ffb9487c-635d-5949-9980-a665c9ff96ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffb9487c-635d-5949-9980-a665c9ff96ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472577} :answer-field/id #uuid "21b67704-7d9c-5f26-8cd4-1364e0247ba7" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473683} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "428e1b82-e6da-5eab-9b6a-0c2af131b494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472578} {:answer/id #uuid "d54291b6-7ef2-55e0-96fb-76e1195bcd41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472579}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "428e1b82-e6da-5eab-9b6a-0c2af131b494" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472578} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa69a530-25df-51d2-8915-d0fef8fbe9e6" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473684}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8076c0f-174e-5d1d-bedf-553b0bcc1d94" :question/math-academy-id "q-127969" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are logically equivalent?\n\nI. $¬Q∧(P∨Q)$\n\nII. $P∧¬Q$\n\nIII. $¬P∧¬Q$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Two statements are logically equivalent if they have the same truth table. So, we construct a truth table for the three given statements:\n\n| $\\,P\\,$ | $\\,Q\\,$ | $\\,¬Q∧(P∨Q)\\,$ | $\\,P∧¬Q\\,$ | $\\,¬P∧¬Q\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ |\n| $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{F}$ | $\\text{T}$ |\n\nWe see that only the statements $¬Q∧(P∨Q)$ and $P∧¬Q$ have the same truth table, so they are logically equivalent.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :step/_content [{:activity/_steps {:activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050777 :topic/id #uuid "65e4f73e-43a8-5089-914d-135e7396b995" :topic/math-academy-id 2821 :topic/title "Logical Equivalence"} :activity/title "Logical Equivalence" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420440} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186420442}]} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411286 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105769 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32467c2c-5605-50e9-b961-a29535a0effb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048819 :topic/id #uuid "c29ac9c3-f2c1-572b-9251-f82101c5a30e" :topic/math-academy-id 1375 :topic/title "The Eigenvalues and Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee9114a1-aa7f-5df3-814e-651d0530d5ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473326} {:answer/id #uuid "032cba6f-622b-5534-9122-a6d2e0064c5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee9114a1-aa7f-5df3-814e-651d0530d5ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473326} :answer-field/id #uuid "86c73323-d68f-52e7-bb08-f5a7c5c876ad" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474058} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ef3ca8-ce71-5b0e-9160-127af09d7f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473328} {:answer/id #uuid "2b7e1916-223f-548d-9df4-0fcfee646ccd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473329}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20ef3ca8-ce71-5b0e-9160-127af09d7f2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473328} :answer-field/id #uuid "55a5184c-a43a-5418-856c-b2919b5f0f7f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474059} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b23bec9-7f3a-5e08-adee-f648ba08bc5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473330} {:answer/id #uuid "c58c188c-bab8-504b-94fd-1ab2f97a5ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473331}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c58c188c-bab8-504b-94fd-1ab2f97a5ab8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473331} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c1a60f6-a246-55e5-8725-fa3150b13681" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0ddf6872-c9fc-59bb-a8a4-f3c287d11e0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-75724" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =1.$\n- **II.** $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =5.$\n- **III.** If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-3,$ then $2v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =-3.$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-1$ (but not $1$).\n\n- Statement II is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{v}_{1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =-4{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=-4v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-4$ (but not $5$).\n\n- Statement III is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(2v)=2Av=2(-3v)=-3(2v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(2v)=-3(2v),$ and $2v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $2v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =-3.$\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8479a60b-b5db-5ad4-8100-fd2db8002091" :knowledge-point/title "Using the x R y Notation" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050648 :topic/id #uuid "5c32a7e5-b594-532c-868b-817b3d0ee933" :topic/math-academy-id 2817 :topic/title "Relations on Finite Sets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81eac6d8-a430-574b-a73a-7693bdbf9561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472886} {:answer/id #uuid "e86bb8e8-1da8-5c04-a45b-a4be5b0a9e58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81eac6d8-a430-574b-a73a-7693bdbf9561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472886} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8bc59ab-a020-5283-b995-4b3f01b96792" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473838} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5871897b-5bfc-587f-a860-d3f937f45f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472888} {:answer/id #uuid "3d525756-0d92-5c9f-ad36-67c13bf7e9ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472889}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5871897b-5bfc-587f-a860-d3f937f45f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472888} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0415415-2a3d-5687-a51f-4a9cd8a2ce8f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473839} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "112fcfe9-f163-5402-bb4c-1c126bca51d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472890} {:answer/id #uuid "448edde1-a526-52c0-ba25-3145d1c46f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "448edde1-a526-52c0-ba25-3145d1c46f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472891} :answer-field/id #uuid "db9f241c-836d-5fde-9168-5d59cd8ab428" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2006d399-9a69-519f-8bb4-45f612aefe6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-213141" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={{0},{0,1}}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nX\\,R\\,Y\\,⇔\\,X⊃Y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${0}\\,R\\,{0,1}$\n- **II.** ${0,1}\\,R\\,{0,1}$\n- **III.** ${0}\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,{0}$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊂{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $X\\,R\\,Y$ if and only if $X⊃Y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $⊃$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $⊅$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $⊃,$ we have ${0}⊃{0,1}.$ This is equivalent to ${0,1}⊂{0},$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $⊃,$ we have ${0,1}⊃{0,1}.$ This is equivalent to ${0,1}⊂{0,1},$ which is false.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $⊅,$ we have ${0}⊅{0}.$ This is equivalent to ${0}⊄{0},$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8479a60b-b5db-5ad4-8100-fd2db8002091" :knowledge-point/title "Using the x R y Notation" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050648 :topic/id #uuid "5c32a7e5-b594-532c-868b-817b3d0ee933" :topic/math-academy-id 2817 :topic/title "Relations on Finite Sets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d941faec-e94f-51c2-a015-6e9922022b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453700} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf7c851-e652-5e8e-8a5f-aea78dae8d9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453701}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cf7c851-e652-5e8e-8a5f-aea78dae8d9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453701} :answer-field/id #uuid "33241716-9c2f-5527-9caf-28ca41203246" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454600} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d75d6d2-7386-5acc-ad1a-1337eec447d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453702} {:answer/id #uuid "5e5651d3-07d9-555a-b807-433068b6fa9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453703}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d75d6d2-7386-5acc-ad1a-1337eec447d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453702} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb50187f-3fd4-55dd-96b1-59e3e5d83fb4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454601} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9171ea78-358f-5962-92c7-ee1e5b63f94e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453704} {:answer/id #uuid "b1018595-c01c-5712-a26d-4c1d47390d3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9171ea78-358f-5962-92c7-ee1e5b63f94e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453704} :answer-field/id #uuid "6937c55d-93a2-573c-bbc0-83804147eb3e" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454602}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5fbac889-73d0-5b8f-a301-da4ef8cb327a" :question/math-academy-id "q-213127" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={1,3}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x=y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $1\\,R\\,1$\n- **II.** $1\\,R\\,3$\n- **III.** $3\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,3$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x=y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $=$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $\\ne $ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is  true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $=$, we have $1=1,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $=,$ we have $1=3,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement III is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $\\ne ,$ we have $3\\ne 3,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411416 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8479a60b-b5db-5ad4-8100-fd2db8002091" :knowledge-point/title "Using the x R y Notation" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050648 :topic/id #uuid "5c32a7e5-b594-532c-868b-817b3d0ee933" :topic/math-academy-id 2817 :topic/title "Relations on Finite Sets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5be660f9-b1a6-5c44-a236-1528e3ac5e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453706} {:answer/id #uuid "633efbab-1e9c-519d-8c87-cf31633aa603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453707}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5be660f9-b1a6-5c44-a236-1528e3ac5e2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453706} :answer-field/id #uuid "efb6a831-307e-5cc1-8685-62da4be3c7f2" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454603} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe94c89-0c24-5c68-b631-7696e034ff88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453708} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b2dd14-66fb-53c2-8cc1-84b717d05aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453709}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9b2dd14-66fb-53c2-8cc1-84b717d05aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453709} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8465c33-c520-5083-bc54-d81a74f17a81" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454604} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f47a411-e333-573c-987e-13415220e695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453710} {:answer/id #uuid "17e30408-772b-5358-b7a6-03830f24a9aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17e30408-772b-5358-b7a6-03830f24a9aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453711} :answer-field/id #uuid "b62f52a8-c0a4-55c9-8e09-ccce1ff6f34f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "77fae439-40b7-5f50-94ed-61df5583b377" :question/math-academy-id "q-213144" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={2,4,6}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x∤y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2\\,R\\,4$\n- **II.** $4\\,R\\,6$\n- **III.** $2\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,6$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∤y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $∤$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $∣$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∤$, we have $2∤4,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∤,$ we have $4∤6,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $2∣6,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f77177ee-74b0-5681-92a8-ed6994ab9f9c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements of Standard Relations" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050648 :topic/id #uuid "5c32a7e5-b594-532c-868b-817b3d0ee933" :topic/math-academy-id 2817 :topic/title "Relations on Finite Sets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "858d1b5f-5918-5ac9-99e7-cf79a2a46733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472868} {:answer/id #uuid "2533356f-13ee-5d42-ad0e-d1a267e0303d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "858d1b5f-5918-5ac9-99e7-cf79a2a46733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472868} :answer-field/id #uuid "89cfb84f-7bdd-58d3-bfb5-bb04de54defe" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473829} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8e26b1ad-a11f-58c2-b68b-eb2904daa308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472870} {:answer/id #uuid "904056bb-a15e-552e-9d05-d3e6998208bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "904056bb-a15e-552e-9d05-d3e6998208bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472871} :answer-field/id #uuid "876dfd72-f2f4-50c0-a2d6-2e6b60c3e021" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473830} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "989df4ea-589f-56da-b83c-762f6d608713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472872} {:answer/id #uuid "7477b5ed-7893-5893-8195-ef07b8905aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "989df4ea-589f-56da-b83c-762f6d608713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472872} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a92b2f8-77ee-5489-93de-bf8f95ddbcef" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bb1a702e-c18f-5fe7-b6f2-5f1506bc9396" :question/math-academy-id "q-213003" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={{0},{1,0}}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following ordered pairs is an element of the relation \"is **not** a subset of\" on $A?$ \n- **I.** $({0},{1,0})$\n- **II.** $({1,0},{0})$\n- **III.** $({1,0},{1,0})$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x⊈y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our ordered pairs in turn.\n\n- The ordered pair $({0},{1,0})$ is **not** an element of $R$ since ${0}⊆{1,0}.\\,\\times $\n\n- The ordered pair $({1,0},{0})$ is an element of $R$ since ${1,0}⊈{0}.\\,✓$\n\n- The ordered pair $({1,0},{1,0})$ is **not** an element of $R$ since ${1,0}⊆{1,0}.\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17