2186454607} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d922689-7ef7-5604-8706-8851f69b6923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453716} {:answer/id #uuid "f5fc08b8-39cf-571a-8ab5-29a1d31aa564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453717}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d922689-7ef7-5604-8706-8851f69b6923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453716} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7423bc3-695e-500a-99f4-aadd8e9ace7b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3479154-8b81-59b7-8f1a-b3765342f61c" :question/math-academy-id "q-213145" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={1,2}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x∣y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $1\\,R\\,1$\n- **II.** $1\\,R\\,2$\n- **III.** $2\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,2$" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $∣$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $∤$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣$, we have $1∣1,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $1∣2,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $∤,$ we have $2∤2,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157237 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8479a60b-b5db-5ad4-8100-fd2db8002091" :knowledge-point/title "Using the x R y Notation" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050648 :topic/id #uuid "5c32a7e5-b594-532c-868b-817b3d0ee933" :topic/math-academy-id 2817 :topic/title "Relations on Finite Sets"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "062ff4a9-f6fb-5801-b6eb-0952304ea02b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453676} {:answer/id #uuid "339f6035-7726-57fe-9feb-d831cfd2e14c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "062ff4a9-f6fb-5801-b6eb-0952304ea02b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453676} :answer-field/id #uuid "19c33b38-0007-5d28-b460-58e1d578c34c" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454588} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04b3cdf4-e1c7-5c7e-b410-80b0d83eac76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453678} {:answer/id #uuid "a022f5bc-b50e-5603-a9a2-7ec95ca3f08e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a022f5bc-b50e-5603-a9a2-7ec95ca3f08e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453679} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6f89dd2-db15-59b3-b32b-1488129f6c7b" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454589} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69e7b551-366b-59d8-9c02-6a7e454cef7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453680} {:answer/id #uuid "daeb101a-4835-5bc3-9af6-7e569ae5a66b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "daeb101a-4835-5bc3-9af6-7e569ae5a66b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453681} :answer-field/id #uuid "e24b07ec-7334-55b1-8f10-4e4e5199867d" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbdd486a-767d-532e-973f-5e22a4525b04" :question/math-academy-id "q-212638" :question/problem "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={e,\\pi }\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x\\ne y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $e\\,R\\,e$\n- **II.** $e\\,R\\,\\pi $\n- **III.** $\\pi \\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,\\pi $" :question/worked-solution "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x\\ne y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $\\ne $ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $=$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\ne $, we have $e\\ne e,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\ne ,$ we have $e\\ne \\pi ,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $=,$ we have $\\pi =\\pi ,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049632 :topic/id #uuid "06f86dee-da2a-593c-8633-10b2e3189b5c" :topic/math-academy-id 1395 :topic/title "Modeling With the Binomial Distribution"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "250ba09f-57fe-5dd0-975a-d2b468a0d1cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472646} {:answer/id #uuid "9c891df1-6527-5ede-8429-6d73ccfed568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c891df1-6527-5ede-8429-6d73ccfed568" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472647} :answer-field/id #uuid "33c474fb-e664-5c6e-88e5-5aef1be1bdc3" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473718} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3d6894e-adb9-5532-9b30-0021133ed0a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472648} {:answer/id #uuid "46a5c76e-74d2-57eb-8b00-086ad0c9ae19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3d6894e-adb9-5532-9b30-0021133ed0a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472648} :answer-field/id #uuid "729331d4-d482-50dd-a51c-36a8b957618a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473719} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9ace851-fe1b-5fcf-b1c4-fef78345c272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472650} {:answer/id #uuid "937b6b94-240a-5a91-be0e-92a51f8282e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9ace851-fe1b-5fcf-b1c4-fef78345c272" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472650} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf12dfe2-08ac-5fa5-be0e-8055df6e4c3f" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29a87319-221e-52be-a49a-7a7f42765bfa" :question/math-academy-id "q-130357" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n- **I.** The number of students who got an $A$ on a math exam in a class of $25$ students\n- **II.** The number of cards drawn until a red card is obtained\n- **III.** The number of books published by a publishing house in a month\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of students who got an $A$ on a math exam can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $25$ independent experiments.\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. Although drawing a red card can be interpreted as a \"success,\" there is not a fixed number of trials in this experiment.\n\n- Random variable III could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of books published cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411562 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049632 :topic/id #uuid "06f86dee-da2a-593c-8633-10b2e3189b5c" :topic/math-academy-id 1395 :topic/title "Modeling With the Binomial Distribution"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "822df2ab-5c24-5f38-9413-37f5aa1c2991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473464} {:answer/id #uuid "d8996723-d37c-5ead-ae67-07923891567c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "822df2ab-5c24-5f38-9413-37f5aa1c2991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473464} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea73a352-768b-5694-b40a-bde32057f2c1" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474127} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54204ba5-69c1-596e-bf82-5cf06d89016c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473466} {:answer/id #uuid "9f2f0299-8235-5ff0-b3b9-547374fc811a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54204ba5-69c1-596e-bf82-5cf06d89016c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473466} :answer-field/id #uuid "62d0eae5-aa19-5fb9-b9ce-5de251492c2c" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474128} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a20af6e7-cf45-5f7c-b07c-8bccae7503be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473468} {:answer/id #uuid "44d28dcb-0256-5a04-865b-984aeab7ba22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473469}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44d28dcb-0256-5a04-865b-984aeab7ba22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473469} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd426fda-1266-52bf-9a5b-ad1aacb1c6ed" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474129}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46c22425-ee4e-5a56-9d8f-8f1ffa9ada86" :question/math-academy-id "q-84748" :question/problem "Which of the following random variables could be modeled as a binomial distribution?\n- **I.** The ages of $20$ soccer players\n- **II.** The price of a pair of pants in a department store\n- **III.** The number of wins by a basketball team in a $30$-game season if the probability of the team winning any individual game is $55%$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A binomial distribution models the number of successes in a sequence of $n$ independent experiments (where each outcome is either success or failure).\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable I could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The ages cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable II could *not* be modeled as a binomial distribution. The prices cannot be interpreted as \"success\" or \"failure.\"\n\n- Random variable III could be modeled as a binomial distribution. The number of wins in $30$ games can be interpreted as the number of successes in a sequence of $30$ independent experiments.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411567 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "162690db-70b3-54a8-a920-576a484a42d9" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Random Variables that Follow a Binomial Distribution" :topic/_knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049632 :topic/id #uuid "06f86dee-da2a-593c-8633-10b2e3189b5c" :topic/math-academy-id 1395 :topic/title "Modeling With the Binomial Distribution"}]}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10e3af4b-f9ce-5ba6-b0a6-1cd12c611ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473458} {:answer/id #uuid "6f111d65-5eb1-5e5e-ab6d-178cdb7b3a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473459}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f111d65-5eb1-5e5e-ab6d-